e.\n            //         //         // In PREP state, we can just register the `match_mask` (16 bits) for each i.\n            //         //         // But that's 16 * 16 = 256 bits. 16 registers of 16 bits.\n            //         //         // Then in SCAN, we can just check if `taken_mask` overlaps with `partner_mask[i]`.\n            //         //         // \n            //         //         // Let's refine the SCAN algorithm with masks.\n            //         //         // \n            //         //         // State SCAN:\n            //         //         // Loop i from 15 to 0:\n            //         //         //   If !taken[i]:\n            //         //         //     result++\n            //         //         //     taken |= partner_mask[i];\n            //         //         // \n            //         //         // This is efficient! No index decoding needed.\n            //         //         // We just need `partner_mask[i]`.\n            //         //         // `partner_mask[i][j] = 1` if `d[i]*k == d[j]` OR `d[j]*k == d[i]`.\n            //         //         // \n            //         //         // How to compute `partner_mask[i]`?\n            //         //         // `partner_mask[i]` = bitmask of `j` where `d[j] == d[i]*k` OR `d[j]*d[i]/k` (if divisible).\n            //         //         // \n            //         //         // Let's stick to the simple definition: If `d[i]*k == d[j]`, then `j` is a partner.\n            //         //         // We only need to check `d[i]*k == d[j]` because if `d[j]*k == d[i]`, then `d[j] = d[i]/k`, \n            //         //         // and when we process `d[j]`, we will mark `d[i]` as partner.\n            //         //         // Wait, if we iterate `i` from largest to smallest, we only care about `d[i]*k` (which is larger, so won't be processed yet) \n            //         //         // and `d[i]/k` (smaller, already processed or not yet processed? If we iterate 15->0, smaller is processed la